# Refuse to install on a Node version outside package.json's `engines` range
# (^22.22.2) instead of printing a warning nobody reads.
#
# WHAT THIS CATCHES: `npm install` and `npm ci`. Verified on 2026-09-03 against
# npm 10.8.2 — on Node 20.20.0 both fail with "Unsupported engine", naming the
# required and actual versions.
#
# WHAT IT DOES NOT CATCH: `npm run <script>`. Verified in the same session — a
# script ran happily on v20.20.0 with this flag set. So this does NOT make the
# definition-of-done commands safe on their own, and `node -v` before running
# them stays a manual step. See the Node version note in CLAUDE.md.
#
# WHAT IT DOES NOT COVER AT ALL: other package managers. This is npm's file and
# npm's flag. Verified 2026-09-04 on Node 22.23.2 against a fixture declaring
# `engines: ^99.0.0`: npm 10.9.8 refuses with EBADENGINE, and `bun install`
# (1.3.14) completes without a word. pnpm reads .npmrc and documents
# engine-strict, and Yarn Berry reads .yarnrc.yml rather than this file —
# neither was measured here, because neither is installed on this machine. So
# the gate is partial, and a checkout on Yarn or Bun gets none of it.
# docs/decisions.md §21 deliberately does not dictate a package manager; this is
# not a contradiction of that, just a limit of the gate that was unrecorded.
#
# Why it matters at all, given that gap: install is the entry point a new
# checkout and CI both go through, and it is where a wrong runtime is cheapest
# to catch. The two Inrupt packages overlap only on the 22 line, and
# @inrupt/solid-client-authn-core declares ^22.0.0 || ^24.0.0 — see
# docs/versions.md.
engine-strict=true
